'''Henry Dargan McMaster''' (born May 27, 1947) is an American politician and attorney serving since 2017 as the 117th [[governor of South Carolina]]. A member of the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]], he served from 2003 to 2011 as the 50th [[List of Attorneys General of South Carolina|attorney general of South Carolina]] and from 2015 to 2017 as the 91st [[Lieutenant Governor of South Carolina|lieutenant governor of South Carolina]], under Governor [[Nikki Haley]].

Born in [[Columbia, South Carolina]], McMaster graduated from the [[University of South Carolina]] with a [[Bachelor of Arts]]. After earning a [[Juris Doctor]] from the [[University of South Carolina School of Law]], he worked for U.S. Senator [[Strom Thurmond]] in private legal practice and as a federal prosecutor. Appointed [[United States Attorney|United States attorney]] for the [[United States District Court for the District of South Carolina|District of South Carolina]] by President [[Ronald Reagan]] in 1981, he gained attention for investigating South Carolina marijuana smugglers in [[Operation Jackpot (drug investigation)|Operation Jackpot]].

After two unsuccessful statewide campaigns, for U.S. Senate in [[1986 United States Senate election in South Carolina|1986]] and lieutenant governor in [[1990 South Carolina lieutenant gubernatorial election|1990]], McMaster chaired the [[South Carolina Republican Party]] from 1993 to 2002. He was elected attorney general in [[2002 South Carolina Attorney General election|2002]] and reelected in [[2006 South Carolina elections#Attorney general|2006]].<ref>{{cite web |title=McMaster for Governor |url=http://www.henrymcmaster.com/meethenry.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170402082544/http://www.henrymcmaster.com/meethenry.html |archive-date=April 2, 2017 |access-date=April 27, 2017}}</ref> He ran for governor in 2010 but lost to Haley in the Republican primary. In 2011, Haley appointed him to the [[South Carolina Ports Authority]]. McMaster was elected lieutenant governor of South Carolina in 2014. He became governor in 2017 upon Haley's resignation to become [[United States Ambassador to the United Nations|U.S. ambassador to the United Nations]]. He was elected to full term in [[2018 South Carolina gubernatorial election|2018]] after narrowly winning a runoff for the Republican nomination, and reelected in [[2022 South Carolina gubernatorial election|2022]].

On January 29, 2025, McMaster became South Carolina's longest-serving governor.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-01-29 |title=South Carolina's McMaster is now the longest serving governor at the job he loves |url=https://apnews.com/article/south-carolina-governor-henry-mcmaster-98169fee88ec4287087c25f692e9c5ce |access-date=2025-01-30 |website=AP News |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-01-29 |title=The State House Gavel: McMaster becomes SC's longest-serving governor |url=https://www.southcarolinapublicradio.org/sc-news/2025-01-29/the-state-house-gavel-mcmaster-becomes-scs-longest-serving-governor |access-date=2025-01-30 |website=South Carolina Public Radio |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-01-29 |title=Governor McMaster celebrates being the longest tenured governor |url=https://www.postandcourier.com/governor-mcmaster-celebrates-being-the-longest-tenured-governor/collection_79c91718-dea9-11ef-a50b-0f7d962de388.html |access-date=2025-01-30 |website=Post and Courier |language=en}}</ref> He is also the [[List of current United States governors|third-longest serving]] incumbent governor in the United States. On April 17, 2025, President [[Donald Trump]] appointed McMaster to the [[Homeland Security Advisory Council]].<ref>[https://abc45.com/news/nation-world/homeland-security-advisory-council-to-include-fox-news-host-mark-levin-sc-governor Homeland Security Advisory Council to include Fox News host Mark Levin, SC governor" by Ray Lewis] The National News Desk; ABC45; April 18th 2025.</ref>

==Early life==
McMaster was born on May 27, 1947, in [[Columbia, South Carolina]].<ref name="MemberBiio">{{cite web|title=South Carolina Legislature Online – Member Biography: Lieutenant Governor Henry D. McMaster|url=http://www.scstatehouse.gov/member.php?code=1298295299|website=Scstatehouse.gov|publisher=South Carolina Legislative Services Agency|access-date=November 17, 2016}}</ref> He is the eldest son of John Gregg and Ida Dargan (Pet) McMaster. He received a [[Bachelor of Arts]] degree in [[history]] from the [[University of South Carolina]] in 1969, and was a member of the [[Kappa Alpha Order]] fraternity.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-08-31 |title=South Carolina Governor's Race Gets 'Frat-Tastic' - FITSNews |url=https://www.fitsnews.com/2022/08/31/south-carolina-governors-race-gets-frat-tastic/ |access-date=2023-02-26 |language=en-US}}</ref> In 1973, he received a [[Juris Doctor]] from the [[University of South Carolina School of Law]], where he served on the editorial board of the ''[[South Carolina Law Review]]''. Later that year, he was admitted to the Richland County Bar Association of the South Carolina Bar. He served in the [[United States Army Reserve]], receiving an honorable discharge in 1975.<ref name="MemberBiio" />

Upon graduation from law school, McMaster worked as a legislative assistant to U.S. senator [[Strom Thurmond]] in [[Washington, D.C.]], until 1974, when he joined the firm of Tompkins and McMaster. McMaster practiced law for almost 29 years, both as a federal prosecutor and in private practice, representing clients in the state and federal courts, trial and appellate.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.henrymcmaster.com/|title=Henry McMaster for Lieutenant Governor|work=henrymcmaster.com}}</ref>

On April 13, 1993, Thurmond's 22-year-old daughter Nancy was killed by a drunk driver while [[jaywalking]] in Columbia, South Carolina. The driver, Corinne Koenig, immediately phoned McMaster, her attorney, and he was present at the scene as Nancy Thurmond was transported to the hospital. After learning the victim's identity, McMaster realized he had a [[conflict of interest]] and withdrew from the case.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.thestate.com/latest-news/article209778159.html |title= Drunk driver in Five Points killed Strom Thurmond's 'little girl' 25 years ago |date=April 29, 2018 |newspaper=The State |first=Cody |last=Dulaney |access-date=July 6, 2022 }}</ref>

