===United States attorney===
Upon Thurmond's recommendation, President [[Ronald Reagan]] nominated McMaster as [[United States Attorney|United States attorney]] for the [[United States District Court for the District of South Carolina|District of South Carolina]] in 1981—Reagan's first nomination for U.S. attorney. The Senate confirmed McMaster on May 21, 1981.<ref>{{cite book|last1=Ryan|first1=Jason|title=Jackpot: High Times, High Seas, and the Sting That Launched the War on Drugs|date=2012|publisher=Rowman & Littlefield|isbn=978-0-7627-6799-1|page=138|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=AVVBBAAAQBAJ&q=thurmond%20reagan%20mcmaster&pg=PA138}}</ref> He headed the South Carolina Law Enforcement Coordinating Committee from 1981 to 1985.<ref>{{cite web |title=2025 Legislative Manual: The Governor |url=https://www.scstatehouse.gov/man25/04_Gov.pdf |publisher=South Carolina Legislative Services Agency |page=16}}</ref>

During his tenure, McMaster created the federal drug task force [[Operation Jackpot (drug investigation)|Operation Jackpot]] to investigate South Carolina [[cannabis (drug)|marijuana]] smugglers.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Haire|first1=Chris|title=Jason Ryan spins the tale of the Lowcountry's gentlemen dope smugglers in Jackpot|url=http://www.charlestoncitypaper.com/charleston/jason-ryan-spins-the-tale-of-the-lowcountrys-gentlemen-dope-smugglers-in-jackpot/Content?oid=3280499|work=[[Charleston City Paper]]|date=April 20, 2011}}</ref> Operation Jackpot ultimately arrested more than 100 people for crimes related to marijuana and [[hashish]] trafficking. McMaster held numerous press conferences during the operation and gained publicity through interviews and comments. His actions were criticized as political, with journalist Lee Bandy writing, "no one can recall any other U.S. attorney being so public-relations conscious" and noting that McMaster had held more press conferences and news releases than all his predecessors combined.<ref>{{cite book|last1=Ryan|first1=Jason|title=Jackpot: High Times, High Seas, and the Sting That Launched the War on Drugs|date=2012|publisher=Rowman & Littlefield|isbn=978-0-7627-6799-1|page=260|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=AVVBBAAAQBAJ&q=thurmond%20reagan%20mcmaster&pg=PA260}}</ref> McMaster completed his term as U.S. attorney in 1985.<ref>{{cite book |title=Bicentennial Celebration of the United States Attorneys, 1789–1989 |publisher=U.S. Department of Justice |year=1989 |at=District of South Carolina |url=https://www.justice.gov/d9/pages/attachments/2018/02/23/bicentennial_celebration.pdf}}</ref>

===Election bids and state appointments===
In 1986, McMaster won the Republican primary for the [[United States Senate]] against Henry Jordan, 27,695 votes (53.4%) to 24,164 (46.6%). McMaster lost the [[1986 United States Senate election in South Carolina|general election]] to Democratic incumbent [[Fritz Hollings]], 465,511 votes (63.1%) to 262,976 (35.6%). He only managed to carry [[Lexington County, South Carolina|Lexington County]].<ref>{{cite report |title=South Carolina Election Report, 1986–1987 |url=https://scvotes.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/Election_Report_1986-1987.pdf |publisher=South Carolina State Election Commission |pages=14, 22}}</ref> 

In 1990, McMaster ran for lieutenant governor. He defeated Sherry Shealy Martschink in the Republican primary, 49,463 votes (51.46%) to 46,660 (48.54%), but again lost to the Democratic incumbent. He received 309,038 votes (41.19%) to [[Nick Theodore]]'s 440,844 (58.75%).<ref>{{cite report |title=South Carolina Election Report, 1990–1991 |url=https://scvotes.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/Election_Report_1990-1991.pdf |publisher=South Carolina State Election Commission |pages=85, 90}}</ref>

In 1991, Governor [[Carroll A. Campbell Jr.]] appointed McMaster to the state's Commission on Higher Education, and the South Carolina Senate confirmed him. He also served on the board of directors of the nonprofit [[South Carolina Policy Council]] from 1991 to 2003, serving as board chairman from 1992 to 1993.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://thetandd.com/news/haley-appoints-to-sc-ports-authority-board/article_912fcb8a-6484-11e0-8a48-001cc4c03286.html|title=Haley appoints 3 to SC Ports Authority board}}</ref> McMaster left the Ports Authority in 2015.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.scstatehouse.gov/man17/04_Gov.pdf |title=Archived copy |access-date=February 18, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170930095508/http://www.scstatehouse.gov/man17/04_Gov.pdf |archive-date=September 30, 2017 |url-status=dead  }}</ref>

===South Carolina Republican Party chair===
On May 8, 1993, McMaster was elected chairman of the [[South Carolina Republican Party]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.newspapers.com/image/345696698/?terms=Henry+McMaster+saturday&match=13|title=9 May 1993, 5 - The Times and Democrat at Newspapers.com}}</ref> He was reelected by the State Republican Convention in 1996, 1998 and 2000. In this capacity, he also served as a member of the [[Republican National Committee]] from 1993 to 2002. Under McMaster's chairmanship, the Republican Party captured the governorship, several statewide offices and (with party switches) the State House of Representatives in 1994, and finally captured control of the powerful State Senate in 2000. Under McMaster, the South Carolina GOP also ran highly contentious and successful presidential primaries in 1996 (won by [[Bob Dole]]) and 2000 (won by [[George W. Bush]]).{{citation needed|date=November 2016}} On March 28, 2002, McMaster announced his resignation as party chairman so that he could run for attorney general.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.newspapers.com/search/|title=Newspapers.com search|website=Newspapers.com}}</ref>

==Attorney General of South Carolina (2003–2011)==
[[File:Henry McMaster official photo.jpg|thumb|150px|Henry McMaster's official portrait, 2005]]
McMaster placed first in the Republican primary for [[Attorney General of South Carolina|attorney general]], with 126,164 votes (42.41%), ahead of State Senator and former judge Larry Richter and attorney Jon E. Ozmint. He defeated Richter in the runoff, 162,014 votes (55.8%) to 128,271 (44.2%). In the general election, McMaster defeated Democratic attorney and former director of the South Carolina Department of Probation, Parole and Pardon Services [[Stephen K. Benjamin]], 601,931 votes (55.5%) to 482,560 (44.5%) in the 2002 South Carolina attorney general election.<ref>{{cite report |title=South Carolina Election Report, 2002 |url=https://scvotes.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/Election_Report_2002.pdf |publisher=South Carolina State Election Commission |pages=24, 32, 43}} and {{cite report |title=South Carolina Election Report, 2006 |url=https://scvotes.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/Election_Report_2006.pdf |publisher=South Carolina State Election Commission |page=46}}</ref> He was reelected in 2006, unopposed in both the Republican primary and the general election.<ref>{{cite report |title=South Carolina Election Report, 2006 |url=https://scvotes.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/Election_Report_2006.pdf |publisher=South Carolina State Election Commission |page=46}}</ref>

==Run for governor; Ports Authority==
In [[2010 South Carolina gubernatorial election|2010]], McMaster ran for governor, but finished third in the Republican primary with 71,187 votes (16.94%), ahead of Lieutenant Governor [[André Bauer]]'s 52,324 (12.45%) but behind U.S. representative [[Gresham Barrett]]'s 91,461 (21.76%) and state representative [[Nikki Haley]]'s 205,360 (48.86%). He immediately endorsed Haley, who had been the front-runner throughout the race and defeated Barrett in the runoff by a landslide.<ref name="The State"/>

In 2011, Haley appointed McMaster to the [[South Carolina Ports Authority]], succeeding Harry Butler Jr. and was replaced by Kurt D. Grindstaff.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.scstatehouse.gov/sess121_2015-2016/sj15/20150604.htm|title=SENATE JOURNAL 6/4/2015 - South Carolina Legislature Online|website=Scstatehouse.gov}}</ref>

===Campaign finance violation===
On January 6, 2015, the Ethics Commission of South Carolina accused McMaster of accepting about $70,000 in donations to his 2010 campaign for governor, exceeding South Carolina's limit for donations by $51,850.<ref name="postandcourier.com">{{cite news|last=Borden|first=Jeremy|url=http://www.postandcourier.com/article/20150106/PC1603/150109692|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150109025319/http://www.postandcourier.com/article/20150106/PC1603/150109692|url-status=dead|archive-date=January 9, 2015|title=New lieutenant governor faces campaign finance allegations from 2010 race|work=[[The Post and Courier]]|date=January 5, 2015}}</ref> The commission released documents stating that McMaster accepted these extra funds to help settle campaign debt.<ref name="postandcourier.com"/><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.greenvilleonline.com/story/news/politics/2015/01/06/mcmaster-accused-taking-donations-limit/21340429/|title=McMaster accused of taking donations above the limit|date=January 6, 2015|work=[[The Greenville News]]}}</ref> In September 2015, the commission refused to dismiss the complaint and McMaster's attorney indicated McMaster was likely to settle.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://wpde.com/news/local-and-state/ethics-board-refuses-to-dismiss-lt-gov-mcmasters-case-09-16-2015|title=Ethics board refuses to dismiss Lt. Gov. McMaster's case|work=[[WPDE-TV]]|date=September 16, 2015|access-date=February 20, 2016}}</ref> In March 2016, the commission ordered McMaster "to repay $72,700 in excess campaign contributions from his 2010 run for governor and pay a $5,100 fine."<ref>{{cite news|last1=Shain|first1=Andrew|title=SC Lt. Gov. McMaster ordered to repay $72,700 in campaign contributions|url=http://www.thestate.com/news/politics-government/politics-columns-blogs/the-buzz/article66405137.html|work=[[The State (newspaper)|The State]]|date=March 16, 2016}}</ref>

==Lieutenant Governor of South Carolina (2015–2017)==
McMaster filed to run for [[Lieutenant Governor of South Carolina|lieutenant governor]] of South Carolina on March 27, 2014.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Shain|first1=Andrew|title=Henry McMaster, another Nikki Haley ally, running for SC lieutenant governor|url=http://www.thestate.com/news/politics-government/politics-columns-blogs/the-buzz/article13844204.html|work=The State|date=March 27, 2014}}</ref> He received 44% of the vote in a four-way [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]] primary, forcing a runoff between him and Mike Campbell, son of former governor [[Carroll A. Campbell Jr.]]<ref>{{cite news|last1=Self|first1=Jamie|title=McMaster bests Campbell in GOP runoff for lieutenant governor|url=http://www.islandpacket.com/news/state/south-carolina/article33581439.html|work=[[The Island Packet]]|date=June 24, 2014}}</ref> McMaster defeated Campbell with 63.6% of the vote<ref>{{cite web|title=2014 Republican and Democratic Primary Runoff|url=http://www.enr-scvotes.org/SC/52366/135097/en/summary.html|publisher=South Carolina State Election Commission|access-date=November 17, 2016}}</ref> and faced Democratic state representative [[Bakari Sellers]] in the general election. During the campaign, Sellers challenged McMaster to renounce his 30-year membership in Columbia's Forest Lake Country Club, a private country club alleged to exclude black members; in response, McMaster's campaign manager said that the club "[had] no policies of racial discrimination" and that McMaster "would not be a member if it did."<ref name="The State">{{cite news|last1=Shain|first1=Andrew|title=ELECTIONS 2014: Last race for SC lieutenant governor enters last week|url=http://www.thestate.com/news/politics-government/article13903772.html|work=The State|date=October 26, 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last1=Borden|first1=Jeremy|last2=Roldan|first2=Cynthia|title=Sellers urges rival to quit club he says excludes blacks|url=https://www.postandcourier.com/politics/sellers-urges-rival-to-quit-club-he-says-excludes-blacks/article_13e61d76-b556-56ac-bf61-7134d379dc59.html|work=The Post and Courier|date=September 3, 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.thestate.com/news/politics-government/politics-columns-blogs/the-buzz/article13880267.html|title=SC Lt. governor candidates spar over McMaster's club membership}}</ref> On November 4, 2014, McMaster was elected lieutenant governor with 58.8% of the vote.<ref>{{cite web|title=SC – Election Results|url=http://www.enr-scvotes.org/SC/53424/149816/en/summary.html|publisher=South Carolina State Election Commission|access-date=November 17, 2016}}</ref> Upon his inauguration, he succeeded Democrat [[Yancey McGill]].<ref name="The State"/>{{Efn|McGill was a Democrat as lieutenant governor, though he was not elected to the position. He became lieutenant governor after Glen McConnell's resignation.|name=|group=}}

McMaster was elected on a separate ticket from Governor Haley, the last time a lieutenant governor was elected in this manner; as of 2018, candidates for governor and lieutenant governor run on the same ticket.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.live5news.com/story/27277119/ap-henry-mcmaster-elected-lieutenant-governor|title=AP: Henry McMaster elected lieutenant governor|work=[[WCSC-TV]]|agency=[[Associated Press]]|date=November 4, 2014}}</ref> During the 2016 presidential campaign, McMaster was an early and avid supporter of [[Donald Trump]]. He claimed to be the first elected politician in the United States to support Trump. After Trump won the Republican nomination, McMaster delivered the nominating speech at the [[Republican National Convention]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://heavy.com/news/2016/11/henry-mcmaster-nikki-haley-new-south-carolina-governor-donald-trump-who-replaces-is-republican-bio-family-wife-age/|title=Henry McMaster: 5 Fast Facts You Need to Know|first=Daniel S.|last=Levine|date=November 23, 2016|access-date=April 27, 2017}}</ref>

==Governor of South Carolina (2017–present)==
===Appointment and elections===
[[File:National Governors Association (32787551220).jpg|thumb|McMaster meeting with [[John F. Kelly]], the [[United States Secretary of Homeland Security|United States secretary of homeland security]], in February 2017]]
[[File:170506-Z-AS099-019 (34338870810).jpg|thumb|McMaster (center) visits the South Carolina National Guard Air and Ground Expo at [[McEntire Joint National Guard Base]], May 2017]]
[[File:South Carolina National Guard prepares to respond to Hurricane Dorian’s impact to state (48660779981).jpg|thumb|McMaster briefs press on South Carolina's emergency response to [[Hurricane Dorian]], 2019]]
[[File:Boeing 787-10 rollout with President Trump (33109601676).jpg|thumb|President [[Donald Trump]] and Henry McMaster rollout the first [[Boeing 787 Dreamliner#787-10|Boeing 787-10]] at the factory in [[Charleston, South Carolina]].]]
On November 23, 2016, [[President-elect of the United States|President-elect]] [[Donald Trump]] announced his intention to nominate Haley as [[United States Ambassador to the United Nations|Ambassador to the United Nations]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/sc-gov-nikki-haley-tapped-to-be-trumps-un-ambassador/2016/11/23/c1395cb6-b144-11e6-8616-52b15787add0_story.html|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|title=Gov. Nikki Haley tapped to be Trump's U.N. ambassador|date=November 23, 2016|access-date=November 23, 2016|first=Robert|last=Costa}}</ref> On January 24, 2017, the Senate confirmed Haley. Later that day, she resigned as governor and McMaster assumed the governorship. Inaugurated at the age of 69 years and 8 months, McMaster is the oldest person ever to assume the office of governor in South Carolina.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Long|first1=Matt|url=https://www.southcarolinaradionetwork.com/2017/01/24/mcmaster-becomes-south-carolinas-117th-governor/|title=McMaster becomes South Carolina's 117th governor|date=January 24, 2017|access-date=April 27, 2017|work=South Carolina Radio Network|publisher=Learfield News & Ag, LLC}}</ref> During the [[2024 Republican Party presidential primaries]], Trump said he nominated Haley in order for McMaster to become governor.<ref name="TrumpTellsSupporters">{{cite web |last1=Sockol |first1=Matthew |title=Trump tells supporters he nominated Haley to the UN for McMaster to become governor |url=https://abcnews4.com/news/local/trump-tells-supporters-he-appointed-haley-to-the-un-for-mcmaster-to-become-governor-south-carolina-sc-nikki-donald-henry-republican-nomination-primary-caucus-iowa-2024-presidential-election-wciv |website=[[WCIV]] |access-date=July 24, 2024 |date=January 14, 2024}}</ref> McMaster served the remainder of Haley's term, which expired in January 2019.<ref name="Collins1" />

McMaster placed first in the June 2018 Republican gubernatorial primary with 155,072 votes.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2018/06/12/us/elections/results-south-carolina-primary-elections.html|title=South Carolina Primary Election Results|first1=Sarah|last1=Almukhtar|first2=Matthew|last2=Bloch|first3=Jasmine C.|last3=Lee|date=June 12, 2018|access-date=February 5, 2019|website=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref> But since he received 42.3% of the vote, less than a majority, he faced John Warren in a runoff. On June 26, McMaster won the runoff with over 53% of the vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.politico.com/election-results/2018/south-carolina/runoff/|title=South Carolina Election Results 2018: Live primary runoff map by county|website=POLITICO|access-date=February 5, 2019}}</ref> He chose businesswoman [[Pamela Evette]] as his running mate over incumbent lieutenant governor [[Kevin L. Bryant]], who ran against McMaster for the gubernatorial nomination.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.thestate.com/news/politics-government/politics-columns-blogs/the-buzz/article186907638.html|title=Here's SC Gov. Henry McMaster's new running mate}}</ref> Trump endorsed McMaster. McMaster defeated Democratic nominee [[James E. Smith Jr.|James Smith]] in the general election with 54% of the vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.politico.com/election-results/2018/south-carolina/|title=South Carolina Election Results 2018|website=POLITICO|access-date=March 21, 2019}}</ref>

McMaster ran for reelection in [[2022 South Carolina gubernatorial election|2022]] and secured the Republican nomination in the June 14 primary. He defeated the Democratic nominee, [[Joe Cunningham (American politician)|Joe Cunningham]], in the general election.<ref>{{cite web |title=South Carolina Primary Results |url=https://www.cnn.com/election/2022/results/south-carolina/primaries |website=CNNpolitics |publisher=CNN |access-date=15 June 2022}}</ref><ref name=":7">{{Cite web |title=SC Election Results |url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2022/11/08/us/elections/results-south-carolina.html?action=click&pgtype=Article&state=default&module=election-results&context=election_recirc&region=StateNavMenu |website=New York Times}}</ref>

==== Recognition ====
McMaster ranked fifth on the Post and Courier Columbia's 2025 Power List.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Staff |first=Post and Courier Columbia |date=2025-12-16 |title=The Post and Courier Columbia's 2025 Power List: 30 people getting stuff done in the capital city |url=https://www.postandcourier.com/columbia/news/post-and-courier-columbia-power-list-2025/article_5a3e81fd-fee8-4463-a04d-30446c46945d.html |access-date=2025-12-17 |website=Post and Courier |language=en}}</ref>

=== Endorsements ===
McMaster endorsed [[Lindsey Graham]] in the 2026 United States Senate race, and served in a leadership capacity on Graham's campaign team until Graham died on July 11.<ref>Kinnard, Meg (February 4, 2025). "Sen. Tim Scott and South Carolina's governor will chair Lindsey Graham's reelection bid". ''[[Associated Press]]''. Retrieved April 4, 2025.</ref>

===Policies===
====Abortion====
McMaster [[Anti-abortion movements|opposes]] [[abortion]]. On February 18, 2021, he signed a bill into law that would ban most abortions.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|date=February 19, 2021|title=South Carolina abortion law suspended 1 day after passage|url=https://apnews.com/article/columbia-reproductive-rights-henry-mcmaster-south-carolina-courts-465712cb85d76bc7af4106046191c8f4|access-date=February 20, 2021|website=AP NEWS}}</ref> The next day, a federal judge suspended the law until June 2022.<ref name=":0" /> After the [[Supreme Court of the United States|U.S. Supreme Court]] overturned [[Roe v. Wade]], McMaster said he "looks forward to the day we don't have abortions," and that he would sign any anti-abortion bill that came across his desk.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Adcox |first1=Seanna |title=McMaster prefers no abortions in SC as protesters converge on Statehouse |url=https://www.postandcourier.com/columbia/mcmaster-prefers-no-abortions-in-sc-as-protesters-converge-on-statehouse/article_2bae9dae-f6f4-11ec-b530-831c649e4a38.html |publisher=Post and Courrier |access-date=30 July 2022}}</ref>

In 2023, McMaster signed the [[Heartbeat bill]] into law, prohibiting abortion after a heartbeat is detected (usually six weeks into pregnancy).<ref>{{cite web |last1=Kashiwagi |first1=Sydney |title=South Carolina governor signs 6-week abortion bill into law |url=https://www.cnn.com/2023/05/25/politics/south-carolina-abortion-ban-bill/index.html |website=cnn.com |publisher=CNN |access-date=3 June 2023}}</ref> The next day, courts blocked the law; McMaster and Republican lawmakers appealed to the [[South Carolina Supreme Court]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Thompson |first1=Alexander |title=McMaster, lawmakers appeal to state Supreme Court after 6-week abortion ban blocked |url=https://www.postandcourier.com/politics/mcmaster-lawmakers-appeal-to-state-supreme-court-after-6-week-abortion-ban-blocked/article_ec39b916-fca5-11ed-bef6-4b7207ca6d76.html |publisher=The Post and Courier |access-date=3 June 2023}}</ref> On August 23, 2023, the state supreme court overrode the block, restoring the stricter prohibition.<ref name="guard-23aug2023">{{cite news |title=South Carolina's all-male highest court upholds six-week abortion ban |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/aug/23/south-carolina-supreme-court-abortion-ban |access-date=August 23, 2023 |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=August 23, 2023}}</ref>

====Fiscal policies====
[[File:Boeing 787-10 rollout with President Trump (32994747622).jpg|thumb|McMaster speaking in February 2017]]
On February 6, 2017, McMaster's first major action as governor was to request $5.18 billion from President Trump for South Carolina's infrastructure. Trump made no public statement about McMaster's request. Later in February, McMaster announced, "[the] state government is going to have to go on a diet as far as spending." On May 9, 2017, he vetoed a bill that would have raised the state's [[Fuel tax|gas tax]] by 12 cents—the largest tax increase in state history—but the [[South Carolina General Assembly]] overrode his veto the following day.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.postandcourier.com/news/it-s-official-south-carolina-has-a-new-gas-tax/article_8717116e-358c-11e7-990d-0b9882828b60.html|title=It's official: South Carolina has a new gas tax after S.C. House and Senate override Gov. McMaster's veto|last=Brown|first=Andrew|work=The Post and Courier|access-date=May 31, 2017|language=en|date=May 10, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210622194548/http://www.postandcourier.com/news/it-s-official-south-carolina-has-a-new-gas-tax/article_8717116e-358c-11e7-990d-0b9882828b60.html|archive-date=June 22, 2021|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.wltx.com/news/politics/sc-gas-tax-increase-becomes-law-after-senate-overrides-veto/438531355|title=SC Gas Tax Increase Becomes Law After Senate Overrides Veto|work=[[WLTX-TV]]|agency=Associated Press|date=May 10, 2017}}{{Dead link|date=June 2023|fix-attempted=Domain excluded from Wayback Machine. No snapshot in archive.is}}</ref> McMaster said the state had "plenty of money in the system to do all the work on the roads if we would just apply it to the roads that need the work... It's not necessary to put yet another tax on the people of South Carolina."<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.postandcourier.com/news/south-carolina-gov-henry-mcmaster-continues-to-oppose-gas-tax/article_26710978-3415-11e7-b648-27db56a723fa.html|title=S.C. Senate advances gas tax bill with supermajority vote, as McMaster promises veto|first=Andrew|last=Brown|work=The Post and Courier|date=8 May 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230405201912/http://www.postandcourier.com/news/south-carolina-gov-henry-mcmaster-continues-to-oppose-gas-tax/article_26710978-3415-11e7-b648-27db56a723fa.html|archive-date=5 April 2023|url-status=live}}</ref> In 2025, McMaster proposed allocating $1.1 billion to state construction projects, one of the largest single-year infrastructure investments in state history.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sentinel |first=Charleston |date=2026-01-06 |title=McMaster seeks $1.1B to rescue stalled road projects |url=https://charlestonsentinel.com/category/politics/mcmaster-road-funding-proposal-inflation |access-date=2026-01-07 |website=Charleston Sentinel |language=en}}</ref>

In 2023, McMaster appointed Democrat [[Brian J. Gaines]] as [[South Carolina Comptroller General|Comptroller General]] in a recess appointment.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Folks |first1=Will |title=South Carolina Democrats Finally 'Win' A Statewide Office |url=https://www.fitsnews.com/2023/05/15/south-carolina-democrats-finally-win-a-statewide-office/ |website=FITSNews |access-date=21 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230515173454/https://www.fitsnews.com/2023/05/15/south-carolina-democrats-finally-win-a-statewide-office/ |archive-date=15 May 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref>

[[File:President_Trump_Arrives_in_SC_%2848959257297%29.jpg|thumb|right|McMaster, a strong ally of Donald Trump, greets the president as he arrives in South Carolina]]

====Firearms====
McMaster is a strong supporter of the [[Second Amendment to the United States Constitution|Second Amendment to the U.S. Constitution]], the [[National Rifle Association]], and legislation that gives citizens easy access to firearms.<ref>{{cite web |last1=McMaster |first1=Henry |title=Protect our Second Amendment |url=https://henrymcmaster.com/protect-our-second-amendment/ |website=henrymcmaster.com |access-date=3 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230202173846/https://henrymcmaster.com/protect-our-second-amendment/ |archive-date=2 February 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref> When students led a walkout to protest [[Gun violence in the United States|gun violence]] and call for stricter [[gun control]] a month after the [[Stoneman Douglas High School shooting]], McMaster called the walkout "shameful" and said it was a "tricky move" orchestrated by "left-wing groups" that were using the students as "tools" to further their agenda.<ref name=twsGreenville111>Noah Feit, The State, March 15, 2018, Greenville News, [https://www.greenvilleonline.com/story/news/2018/03/15/south-carolina-gov-henry-mcmaster-calls-student-walkout-shameful-political-statement/427196002/ South Carolina Gov. Henry McMaster calls student walkout a 'shameful political statement'], Retrieved March 15, 2018, "...The Republican criticized the event ... called it "shameful," ... orchestrated by a "left-wing group."..."This is a tricky move, I believe, by a left-wing group ... a political statement by a left-wing group and it's shameful."</ref> Student activist [[David Hogg]], who survived the Parkland shooting, criticized McMaster, saying in a tweet that "future voters will not reelect you and outlive you too."<ref name=twsGreenville111/> In 2021, McMaster signed a bill that allows South Carolina's citizens to [[open carry]] a firearm with a [[Concealed carry in the United States#Permitting policies|concealed weapons permit]], which required holders to pass a training course and background check.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Altman-Devilbiss |first1=Alexx |title=Gov. McMaster fires guns to celebrate SC open carry law |url=https://wpde.com/news/local/gov-mcmaster-fires-guns-to-celebrate-sc-open-carry-law |access-date=3 June 2023}}</ref> But that requirement was eliminated after McMaster signed legislation in 2024 that allows permit-less open carry. The law also reduced the minimum age for handgun ownership from 21 to 18 and increased penalties for unlawful uses of firearms.<ref name="Collins1">{{cite web |last1=Collins |first1=Jeffery |title=Law-abiding adults can now carry guns openly in South Carolina after governor approves new law |url=https://apnews.com/article/south-carolina-open-carry-gun-law-governor-2f92147440ee5434560bbb4687855cbc |website=apnews.com |publisher=Associated Press}}</ref>

====Immigration====
In 2018, McMaster offered to send troops from the [[South Carolina National Guard]] to assist [[Texas]] in combating illegal immigration and drug trafficking. This came after Trump called for the deployment of thousands of soldiers along the [[Mexico–United States border]], in which border states such as [[Arizona]] and [[New Mexico]] also participated.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://wach.com/news/local/sc-national-guard-troops-could-be-heading-to-mexican-border-wall|title=SC National Guard troops could be heading to Mexico border wall|last=Goss|first=Jamal|work=[[WACH]]|date=April 10, 2018}}</ref> In 2023, after a request for assistance by Texas governor [[Greg Abbott]] following the expiration of [[Title 42 of the United States Code|Title 42]], McMaster authorized the deployment of South Carolina National Guard troops to Texas.<ref>{{cite web |title=McMaster to send SC National Guard troops to Texas |url=https://www.wltx.com/article/news/national/military-news/governor-henry-mcmaster-to-send-southcarolina-national-guard-troops-to-texas-patrol-border-mexico/101-532f3bf4-091e-4746-98bd-d3fac47c8161 |website=wltx.com |publisher=WLTX |access-date=3 June 2023}}</ref> He called the troop mobilization an attempt to "stop the drug cartels, criminals, and terrorists from entering our country to peddle their poison".<ref>{{cite web |last1=McMaster |first1=Henry |title=Gov. Henry McMaster Directs Deployment of National Guard Troops to Southern Border |url=https://governor.sc.gov/news/2023-05/gov-henry-mcmaster-directs-deployment-national-guard-troops-southern-border |website=governor.sc.gov |publisher=Governor of South Carolina |access-date=3 June 2023}}</ref>

==== Labor unions ====
In 2024, McMaster joined five other Republican governors ([[Kay Ivey]], [[Brian Kemp]], [[Tate Reeves]], [[Bill Lee (Tennessee politician)|Bill Lee]], and [[Greg Abbott]]) in a statement opposing the [[United Auto Workers]] unionization campaign.<ref>{{Cite web |date=April 16, 2024 |title=Governor Henry McMaster, Fellow Republican Governors Issue Joint Statement in Opposition to United Auto Workers Unionization Campaign {{!}} S.C. Governor Henry McMaster |url=https://governor.sc.gov/news/2024-04/governor-henry-mcmaster-fellow-republican-governors-issue-joint-statement-opposition |access-date= |website=governor.sc.gov}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-04-16 |title=Governor Ivey & Other Southern Governors Issue Joint Statement in Opposition to United Auto Workers (UAW)'s Unionization Campaign |url=https://governor.alabama.gov/newsroom/2024/04/governor-ivey-other-southern-governors-issue-joint-statement-in-opposition-to-united-auto-workers-uaws-unionization-campaign/ |access-date= |website=Office of the Governor of Alabama |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Wayland |first=Michael |date=2024-04-16 |title=Republican governors from six states condemn UAW campaigns, citing potential for layoffs |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2024/04/16/republican-governors-condemn-united-auto-workers-campaigns.html |access-date= |website=CNBC |language=en}}</ref>

==== LGBTQ+ issues ====
In 1993, as chair of the South Carolina Republican Party, McMaster criticized a bill cosponsored by Senator [[Fritz Hollings]], a Democrat, that would prohibit [[workplace discrimination based on sexual orientation]]. McMaster said, "I want to know why he sponsored the gay-rights bill that allows homosexuals to be recruited to teach in schools, among other things." In 2006, as attorney general, McMaster successfully led the fight to ban same-sex marriage in South Carolina. He also said that South Carolina should "secede" over [[Don't Ask, Don't Tell]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Joe Cunningham Wants to Know If Henry McMaster Still Opposes Marriage Equality Due to History of Bigoted Remarks |url=https://holycitysinner.com/2022/10/14/joe-cunningham-wants-to-know-if-henry-mcmaster-still-opposes-marriage-equality-due-to-history-of-bigoted-remarks/ |publisher=HolyCitySinner |access-date=8 November 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Lavietes |first1=Matt |title=S.C. governor says marriage should be 'between a man and a woman' at debate |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/nbc-out/out-politics-and-policy/sc-governor-says-marriage-man-woman-debate-rcna54393 |website=NBCnews.com |publisher=NBC |access-date=8 November 2022}}</ref> In an October 2022 gubernatorial debate, McMaster said that if the U.S. Supreme Court overturned ''[[Obergefell v. Hodges]]'', he would enforce South Carolina's preexisting legislation that banned same-sex marriage.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-10-27 |title=Republican governor candidate says he wants to impose a statewide ban on gay marriage |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/us-politics/henry-mcmaster-south-carolina-gay-marraige-b2212167.html |access-date=2022-10-27 |website=The Independent |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Lavietes |first1=Matt |title=S.C. governor says marriage should be 'between a man and a woman' at debate |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/nbc-out/out-politics-and-policy/sc-governor-says-marriage-man-woman-debate-rcna54393 |website=nbcnews |publisher=NBC |access-date=20 November 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title='I'm old-fashioned': South Carolina governor says he does not support gay marriage |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lTRnvAJWpLk |website=youtube.com |publisher=SCETV |access-date=20 November 2022}}</ref> In 2022, he signed a bill that requires student athletes from elementary school to college to play on the team that corresponds to the gender listed on their birth certificates.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Leneau |first1=Delphine |title=Human Rights Campaign Condemns Governor McMaster for Signing South Carolina Anti-Transgender Sports Ban Into Law |url=https://www.hrc.org/press-releases/human-rights-campaign-condemns-governor-mcmaster-for-signing-south-carolina-anti-transgender-sports-ban-into-law |website=hrc.org |publisher=Human Rights Campaign |access-date=3 June 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Aitken |first1=Peter |title=South Carolina governor signs transgender sports bill, competition based on birth certificate |url=https://www.foxnews.com/us/south-carolina-governor-transgender-sports-bill |website=foxnews.com/ |publisher=FOX |access-date=3 June 2023}}</ref>

====COVID-19 pandemic====
{{See also|COVID-19 pandemic in South Carolina}}
[[File:President Donald J. Trump visits North and South Carolina (29868275707) (cropped).jpg|thumb|McMaster and President [[Donald Trump]]]]
Like most governors during the [[COVID-19 pandemic in the United States]] in 2020, McMaster declared a state of emergency. On April 20, 2020, McMaster drew criticism for reopening beaches and retail stores five days after South Carolina experienced its highest number of new positive COVID-19 tests,<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.abccolumbia.com/2020/04/17/business-owners-leaders-react-to-gov-mcmasters-hope-for-businesses-reopening-in-may/|title=Business leaders react to Gov. McMaster's hope for businesses reopening in May| work=ABC Columbia|access-date=April 20, 2020|language=en}}</ref> but he did include additional social distancing requirements in his executive order.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://governor.sc.gov/sites/default/files/Documents/Executive-Orders/2020-04-06%20eFILED%20Executive%20Order%20No.%202020-21%20-%20Stay%20at%20Home%20or%20Work%20Order.pdf|title=Executive Order 2020-21| work=South Carolina Executive Department|access-date=April 20, 2020|language=en}}</ref> McMaster said the economy was stalled because of the pandemic and that he anticipated having the economy "humming" by June 2020.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.thestate.com/news/coronavirus/article242061161.html|title=Gov. McMaster: economy expected to be "humming, back in business," by June's end| work=The State|access-date=April 20, 2020|language=en}}</ref> By early June, McMaster reopened the majority of the state, though schools remained closed and businesses were required to follow social distancing procedures.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.greenvilleonline.com/story/news/local/2020/05/28/mcmaster-defends-handling-pandemic-day-after-20-covid-19-deaths/5278549002/|title=A day after 20 COVID-19 deaths, Gov. McMaster says SC is charting proper path for pandemic|last=Brown|first=Kirk|date=May 28, 2020|publisher=Greenville News|access-date=June 2, 2020}}</ref> Many schools and universities across the country canceled graduation ceremonies,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.thedenverchannel.com/news/national/coronavirus/graduation-ceremonies-canceled-postponed-amid-covid-19-outbreak|title=Graduation ceremonies canceled, postponed amid COVID-19 outbreak|last=Taylor|first=Annie|date=March 23, 2020|publisher=ABC Denver|access-date=June 16, 2020}}</ref> but McMaster urged South Carolina schools to hold ceremonies in a manner that abided by social distancing requirements.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.live5news.com/2020/04/22/mcmaster-spearman-make-announcement-school-year-plans/|title=Gov. McMaster: S.C. public schools to remain closed for remainder of school year|last=Phillips|first=Patrick|date=April 22, 2020|publisher=WCSC5|access-date=June 16, 2020}}</ref> He delivered the commencement address at [[West Florence High School]]'s graduation ceremony and sang "[[Mull of Kintyre (song)|Mull of Kintyre]]" while playing his guitar.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.wltx.com/article/features/henry-mcmaster-sings-plays-guitar/101-733464e1-3614-4c62-8718-09187ceb0875|title=Gov. Henry McMaster plays guitar, sings at high school graduation|date=June 1, 2020|publisher=WLTX|access-date=June 16, 2020}}</ref>

On June 10, state epidemiologist [[Linda Bell (epidemiologist)|Linda Bell]] said that COVID-19 was worse in June than it was when the state was closed in March and April. On average, the number of cases in early June was five to six times higher than in March and April. McMaster said, "it is up to the people to determine what kind of precautions need to be followed," indicating that he would not place any more restrictions on businesses and public facilities.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.live5news.com/2020/06/10/mcmaster-state-health-officials-holding-presser-covid-response/|title=McMaster will not close South Carolina businesses following increase of daily COVID-19 cases |last=Rivera|first=Ray|date=June 10, 2020|publisher=5 WCSC News|access-date=June 13, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.wspa.com/news/state-news/gov-mcmaster-declares-new-state-of-emergency-lifts-restrictions-on-bowling-alleys-retail-capacity-restraints/|title=Gov. McMaster declares new State of Emergency; lifts restrictions on bowling alleys, retail capacity restraints|date=June 12, 2020|publisher=WCBD|access-date=June 13, 2020}}</ref> Bell accused McMaster of intentionally misleading the public about her stance on reopening the economy; she claimed that his staff was "somewhat manipulative"<ref name=":6">{{Cite web|last=Fretwell|first=Sammy|date=August 10, 2020|title=Governor's office misled public about DHEC doctor's coronavirus stance, she says|url=https://www.thestate.com/news/coronavirus/article244821717.html|access-date=August 10, 2020|website=The State}}</ref> and made it appear that she and other scientists backed his plan to reopen the economy when she did not.<ref name=":6" />

By late June and early July, confirmed cases in South Carolina had risen by as much as 2,000% compared to March and April.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Mallory|first=Laurel|date=July 2, 2020|title=SC reports 1,629 new COVID-19 cases and 19 more deaths Thursday|url=https://www.wistv.com/2020/07/02/sc-reports-new-covid-cases-more-deaths-thursday/|access-date=June 2, 2020|website=WISTV}}</ref> Despite pleas from lawmakers and other state officials, McMaster declined to enact a statewide requirement to wear masks, saying that such an order would be unenforceable.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web|last=Bohatch|first=Emily|date=July 1, 2020|title=McMaster refuses to create SC mask policy, so local governments acted on their own|url=https://www.thestate.com/news/coronavirus/article243870277.html|access-date=July 2, 2020|website=The State}}</ref> Consequently, dozens of municipalities enacted their own mask requirement ordinances, including [[Columbia, South Carolina|Columbia]], [[Forest Acres, South Carolina|Forest Acres]], [[Greenville, South Carolina|Greenville]], [[Charleston, South Carolina|Charleston]], [[Lexington, South Carolina|Lexington]], and most large cities and towns.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web|date=July 2, 2020|title=Where do you have to wear a mask in SC? A look at face mask ordinances in place across the state|url=https://www.wyff4.com/article/where-do-you-have-to-wear-a-mask-in-sc-a-look-at-face-mask-ordinances-in-place-across-the-state/33010402#|access-date=July 2, 2020|website=WYFF}}</ref> Bell criticized McMaster's decision and said a statewide mask requirement would be more effective. McMaster responded that a statewide requirement would give a “false sense of security to South Carolinians."<ref name=":1" /> [[List of Attorneys General of South Carolina|Attorney General]] [[Alan Wilson (politician)|Alan Wilson]] noted that ordinances and laws requiring masks are not illegal.<ref name=":2" /> On July 10, as the state's COVID-19 cases exceeded 50,000 and nearly 900 deaths,<ref>{{Cite web|date=July 9, 2020|title=1,723 new confirmed SC virus cases, 22 additional confirmed deaths|url=https://www.wltx.com/article/news/health/coronavirus/sc-dhec-coronavirus-covid19-numbers-july9-2020-southcarolina/101-1f5e12d7-2a36-4cbb-add6-ba5ac91cf509|access-date=July 10, 2020|website=WLTX}}</ref> McMaster issued an executive order prohibiting businesses from selling alcohol after 11 p.m. in an effort to prevent the spread of the virus among younger people.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Bustos|first=Joseph|date=July 10, 2020|title=McMaster restricts SC alcohol sales to slow spread of COVID-19 among young people|url=https://www.thestate.com/news/coronavirus/article244135712.html|access-date=July 10, 2020|website=The State}}</ref>

On July 15, McMaster said in an address that South Carolina schools should give parents the option to send students to in-person classes in September 2020. He instructed [[South Carolina Department of Education|Superintendent of Education]] Molly Spearman to reject any school's reopening plan if it did not allow in-person learning.<ref>{{Cite web|date=July 15, 2020|title=SC Gov. Henry McMaster recommends schools reopen after Labor Day|url=https://www.wyff4.com/article/back-to-school-in-south-carolina-governor-legislative-leaders-discuss-reopening-at-11-am/33321652|website=WYFF4}}</ref><ref name=":3">{{Cite web|last=Gilreath|first=Ariel|date=July 15, 2020|title=SC superintendent and teachers push back on governor's direction for in-person classes|url=https://www.greenvilleonline.com/story/news/2020/07/15/sc-education-superintendent-pushes-back-governors-direction/5442495002/|access-date=July 15, 2020|website=Greenville News}}</ref> Groups such as the [[Palmetto State Teachers Association]], the S.C. Education Association, and [[SC for Ed]] criticized McMaster's order, saying that it "would needlessly jeopardize the health and safety of our state's 800,000 students and more than 50,000 teachers."<ref>{{Cite web|last=Glover|first=Emery|date=July 15, 2020|title=Top SC education advocacy groups all oppose governor's push to reopen schools|url=https://www.wistv.com/2020/07/15/top-sc-education-advocacy-groups-all-oppose-governors-push-reopen-schools/|access-date=July 15, 2020|website=WISTV}}</ref> Several school districts, including [[Greenville County School District]], the state's largest, denounced the order.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Gilreath|first=Ariel|date=July 15, 2020|title=Greenville County Schools denounces governor's push to reopen schools 5 days a week|url=https://www.greenvilleonline.com/story/news/2020/07/15/greenville-county-schools-denounces-henry-mcmaster-statements-open-sc-schools-sept-8-coronavirus/5444397002/|access-date=July 15, 2020|website=Greenville News}}</ref> Spearman, a [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] elected independently of McMaster, also disapproved of his order.<ref name=":3" />

On July 29, McMaster signed an executive order requiring face masks for employees and patrons in all South Carolina restaurants and bars and prohibiting any restaurant from operating at more than 50% capacity, seating tables less than six feet apart, or allowing people to congregate at a bar.<ref>{{Cite news|date=July 29, 2020|title=Gov. McMaster announces new restrictions, new openings for businesses in South Carolina|work=4WYFF|url=https://www.wyff4.com/article/live-at-4-sc-gov-henry-mcmaster-health-officials-give-coronavirus-update/33459266#|access-date=August 3, 2020}}</ref> By October, McMaster lifted most restrictions and unveiled the SC Coronavirus Aid, Relief, and Economic Security (CARES) Act grants, a $65 million program aimed at helping small businesses and minority-owned businesses affected by COVID-19.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Spiker|first=Chris|date=October 23, 2020|title=Gov. McMaster visits Myrtle Beach, discusses new $65 million program for small business hurt by COVID-19|url=https://www.wbtw.com/news/grand-strand/gov-mcmaster-visits-myrtle-beach-discusses-new-65-million-program-for-small-business-hurt-by-covid-19/|website=WBTW News}}</ref>

On December 22, the governor's office announced that McMaster and wife had both tested positive for COVID-19.<ref>{{Cite news|date=December 22, 2020|title=Gov. Henry McMaster tests positive for COVID-19|work=WYFF|url=https://www.wyff4.com/article/gov-henry-mcmaster-tests-positive-for-covid-19-coronavirus/35046594|access-date=December 22, 2020}}</ref>

In 2021, McMaster said he would block the federal government from sending people door-to-door to promote vaccinations.<ref>{{Cite web|date=July 26, 2021|title=How Red States Got Their Groove Back|url=https://www.governing.com/now/how-red-states-got-their-groove-back|access-date=July 27, 2021|website=Governing|language=en}}</ref> In September 2021, he criticized federal vaccine requirements, saying, "Biden and the radical Democrats [have] thumbed their noses at the Constitution."<ref>{{Cite web|date=September 9, 2021|title=Sweeping new vaccine mandates for 100 million Americans|url=https://apnews.com/article/joe-biden-business-health-coronavirus-pandemic-executive-branch-18fb12993f05be13bf760946a6fb89be|access-date=September 10, 2021|website=AP NEWS|language=en}}</ref> McMaster pledged to fight Biden "to the gates of hell" over the vaccine requirement.<ref>{{Cite web|title=SC governor vows to fight Biden 'to the gates of hell' after vaccine mandates speech|url=https://www.wltx.com/article/news/health/coronavirus/south-carolina-governor-henry-mcmaster-gates-of-hell/101-92fb1dd6-6df0-4dc6-9903-54db40bbe5b9|access-date=September 10, 2021|website=wltx.com|date=September 9, 2021 |language=en-US}}</ref> In [[Measles resurgence in the United States#2025-2026 South Carolina outbreak|2025–2026]], South Carolina was ground zero for the largest measles outbreak since 2000, the year in which measles were declared eliminated in the United States.<ref name=":8">{{Cite news |last=Hartocollis |first=Anemona |last2=Gutierrez |first2=Kaoly |date=2026-03-03 |title=In South Carolina Measles Outbreak, Vaccine Skepticism Led to Largest Outbreak Since 2000 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/03/us/politics/south-carolina-measles-outbreak-vaccines.html |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> The outbreak was attributed to low vaccination rates but McMaster did not support revising South Carolina's vaccination exemption policies.<ref name=":8" />

==== George Floyd protests ====
{{See also|George Floyd protests}}
In response to the murder of [[George Floyd]] in [[Minnesota]], protests flared across the nation. In South Carolina, protests occurred in [[Columbia, South Carolina|Columbia]], [[Greenville, South Carolina|Greenville]], and [[Charleston, South Carolina|Charleston]]. Greenville's protests were largely peaceful, but there were some instances of violence in Columbia and Charleston. In Columbia, police cars were destroyed, shots were fired at police, and businesses were vandalized. In Charleston, protesters stopped traffic on Interstate 26 and the [[Arthur Ravenel Jr. Bridge]].<ref>{{cite web|last=Tripp|first=Drew|date=May 30, 2020|title=Protesters block I-26 in Charleston|url=https://abcnews4.com/news/local/protesters-block-i-26-in-charleston|access-date=June 2, 2020|publisher=ABC4 News}}</ref> Like President Trump, McMaster said that state and local governments were being too lenient on rioters and looters and was critical of Charleston's initial response. He said to Trump, "We've got to take people out, give them justice, make it more than just the cost of doing business to come into one town, get arrested, pay bond."<ref>{{cite web |url=https://abcnews4.com/news/local/mcmaster-critical-of-charlestons-intial-response-to-riots-looting-in-call-with-trump|title=McMaster critical of Charleston's initial response to riots, looting in call with Trump|last=Tripp|first=Drew|date=June 2, 2020|publisher=ABC4 News|access-date=June 2, 2020}}</ref> McMaster mobilized the [[South Carolina National Guard]] to be dispatched, if necessary, to halt riots and looting. At Trump's request, he also sent members of the National Guard to [[Washington, D.C.]] to aid in larger-scale protests.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.thestate.com/news/local/military/article243200241.html|title= South Carolina National Guard soldiers headed to Washington at Trump's request|last=Feit|first=Noah|date=June 2, 2020|publisher=The State|access-date=June 2, 2020}}</ref>

=== Allegations and investigations ===
====Corruption investigation====
{{see also|South Carolina Statehouse corruption investigation}}

In 2017, McMaster, the University of South Carolina, [[BlueCross BlueShield of South Carolina|BlueCross BlueShield]], Columbia Mayor [[Steve Benjamin (politician)|Steve Benjamin]], and a number of prominent state legislators were connected to [[Richard Quinn (political consultant)|Richard Quinn]] and Associates, a firm he employed for political consulting.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.postandcourier.com/news/quinns-fight-back-against-south-carolina-statehouse-corruption-probe/article_e8203f4e-3f3c-11e7-b12d-7b900ff42f2e.html|title=Quinns fight back against South Carolina Statehouse corruption probe|last=Shain|first=Andy|work=The Post and Courier|access-date=May 31, 2017|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.postandcourier.com/news/statehouse-corruption-investigators-seek-information-from-bluecross-blueshield-of-south/article_9fb510b8-0fe4-11e7-be5a-47140a92cd1b.html|title=Statehouse corruption investigators seek information from BlueCross BlueShield of South Carolina|last=Shain|first=Andy|work=The Post and Courier|access-date=May 31, 2017|language=en}}</ref> Quinn was named as part of a larger corruption probe Special Prosecutor [[David Pascoe]] conducted within the South Carolina General Assembly, which first ensnared then-speaker [[Bobby Harrell]], who resigned and pleaded guilty to public corruption charges in 2014.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.thestate.com/news/politics-government/article137924333.html|title=The 'Quinndom' and the power|work=The State|access-date=April 27, 2017}}</ref> McMaster has not been implicated in Pascoe's corruption probe, but four state legislators have been indicted as part of it as of May 2017.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.thestate.com/news/politics-government/article150867702.html|title=Indicted Rep. Rick Quinn once led House GOP, defended Confederate flag|work=The State|access-date=May 31, 2017|language=en}}</ref>

McMaster's connections to Richard Quinn and Associates caused him difficulty in the South Carolina legislature when he attempted to replace two members of the [[South Carolina Ports Authority]] (SCPA) Board of Directors whose terms had expired. At the time of McMaster's replacement nominations, the SCPA paid Quinn a consulting fee of $8,100 per month.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.postandcourier.com/business/gov-henry-mcmaster-wants-to-replace-ports-board-members-who/article_28b17e50-0e3d-11e7-8b0f-6bb0d5207c9a.html|title=Gov. Henry McMaster wants to replace Ports board members who questioned payments to embattled political consultant Richard Quinn|first=David|last=Wren|work=The Post and Courier|access-date=April 27, 2017}}</ref> State lawmakers delayed the vote on McMaster's two nominees for two weeks, citing the ongoing corruption probe that had pulled in three Republican legislators.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.postandcourier.com/news/corruption-probe-prompts-south-carolina-lawmakers-to-delay-vote-on/article_acd4469a-0f0f-11e7-9f59-7f82a7c785e1.html|title=Corruption probe prompts South Carolina lawmakers to delay vote on Gov. McMaster's Ports Authority nominees|first=Andrew|last=Brown|work=The Post and Courier|access-date=April 27, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.postandcourier.com/news/lawmakers-advance-south-carolina-gov-henry-mcmaster-s-ports-board/article_a4fc357e-193a-11e7-99b2-fbcaca7e08e7.html|title=Lawmakers advance South Carolina Gov. Henry McMaster's ports board picks|last=Brown|first=Andrew|work=The Post and Courier|access-date=May 31, 2017|language=en}}</ref> Both nominees were confirmed after the SCPA voted to cease employing Quinn.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.postandcourier.com/business/south-carolina-ports-agency-suspends-payments-to-consultant-richard-quinn/article_c48faa26-149c-11e7-9964-bfd3d7792261.html|title=South Carolina Ports agency suspends payments to consultant Richard Quinn during Statehouse corruption probe|last=Wren|first=David|work=The Post and Courier|access-date=May 31, 2017|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.scspa.com/about/mission-and-leadership/board-of-directors/|title=Board of Directors|website=SC Ports Authority|date=December 2015 |access-date=May 31, 2017}}</ref> McMaster likewise ceased to use Quinn in advance of his 2018 gubernatorial campaign.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.postandcourier.com/news/south-carolina-gov-henry-mcmaster-not-using-embattled-political-consultant/article_c919b99a-372b-11e7-94ed-cf40a27f4654.html|title=South Carolina Gov. Henry McMaster not using embattled political consultant Richard Quinn in 2018 race|last=Shain|first=Andy|work=The Post and Courier|access-date=May 31, 2017|language=en}}</ref>

==== Nukegate ====
{{See also|Nukegate scandal}}
McMaster was in office when it was announced that [[SCANA]] and [[Santee Cooper]] were abandoning the expansion of the [[Virgil C. Summer Nuclear Generating Station]] in 2017. The event has been nicknamed the [[Nukegate scandal]].<ref>{{Cite news|title=South Carolina attorney general: Law that allowed 'NukeGate' to happen is unconstitutional|url=https://www.washingtontimes.com/news/2017/sep/26/south-carolina-attorney-general-alan-wilson-law-th/|access-date=December 27, 2020|work=The Washington Times|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=FITSNews|title=South Carolina's NukeGate Investigation: Former SCANA CEO Pleading Guilty|date=November 24, 2020 |url=https://www.fitsnews.com/2020/11/24/south-carolinas-nukegate-investigation-former-scana-ceo-pleads-guilty/|access-date=December 27, 2020|language=en-US}}</ref> Following the retirement of Santee Cooper's chairman, McMaster appointed [[Charlie Condon]] as a replacement.<ref>{{Cite web|last=AP|date=March 8, 2018|title=Governor taps ex-prosecutor to lead South Carolina utility|url=https://wach.com/news/local/governor-taps-ex-prosecutor-to-lead-south-carolina-utility|access-date=December 27, 2020|website=WACH}}</ref> McMaster favors the sale of the utility, which he has called a "rogue agency" due to its independence and financial problems.<ref>Santee Cooper's Future Back for Debate at the State, NPR State House Week. February 14, 2020. Retrieved from https://www.npr.org/podcasts/406774343/state-house-week on December 24, 2020.</ref>

====''Adams v. Henry McMaster''====
McMaster is a longtime advocate of private education. In 2020, as part of the Governor's Emergency Education Relief (GEER) and the CARES Act, federal funds were distributed to support education in states amid the [[COVID-19 pandemic]]. McMaster announced that he sought to use $32 million, or two thirds, of South Carolina's $48 million allotment on private [[school vouchers]], and that 5,000 grants to private schools would be made with the federal relief money.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Phillips|first=Patrick|date=July 20, 2020|title=Gov. McMaster announces SAFE Grants program to help families afford tuition for private schools|url=https://www.live5news.com/2020/07/20/gov-mcmaster-make-announcement-education-relief-funds/|access-date=July 22, 2020|website=5WCSC}}</ref> His decision received harsh criticism from educators and politicians around the state.<ref name=":4">{{Cite web|last=Kalsi|first=Dal|date=July 21, 2020|title=SC teacher, school associations respond to McMaster's SAFE Grants Education Program to help private school students in SC|url=https://www.foxcarolina.com/news/sc-teacher-school-associations-respond-to-mcmasters-safe-grants-education-program-to-help-private-school/article_b8ce31fa-ca6d-11ea-b30e-6f53b440f77e.html|access-date=July 22, 2020|website=Fox Carolina}}</ref> The Palmetto State Teachers Association stated that the money could have been better used for South Carolina's nearly 1,000,000 students in public education instead of 5,000 students in private schools. [[SC for Ed]], a teachers' advocacy group, called the decision "disappointing."<ref name=":4" /><ref>{{Cite web|last=Towers|first=Stephanie|date=July 20, 2020|title=Groups share strong opposition to SC governor's use of CARES Act funds for private schooling|url=https://www.wyff4.com/article/groups-share-strong-opposition-to-sc-governors-use-of-cares-act-funds-for-private-schooling/33369883#|access-date=July 20, 2020|website=4WYFF}}</ref>

McMaster's supporters argued that parents should have a choice about where to send their children. On July 22, 2020, McMaster's decision was halted by court order after educators sued him for failing to uphold a principle of the [[South Carolina Constitution]]: "No money shall be paid from public funds nor shall the credit of the State or any of its political subdivisions be used for the direct benefit of any religious or other private educational institution."<ref>{{Cite web|date=July 22, 2020|title=SC court issues order against McMaster's allocation of $32 million to private schools|url=https://www.wltx.com/article/news/education/sc-judge-files-order-against-mcmasters-allocation-of-32-million-to-private-schools/101-60e837a8-55fe-4655-9517-f8ee4ce3c424|access-date=July 22, 2020|website=WLTX}}</ref><ref name=":5">{{Cite news|last=Lovegrove|first=Jamie|date=July 29, 2020|title=No ruling yet in lawsuit over Gov. McMaster's private-school grant program|work=The Post and Courier|url=https://www.postandcourier.com/politics/no-ruling-yet-in-lawsuit-over-gov-mcmasters-private-school-grant-program/article_fff5e2aa-d1cd-11ea-ae4d-039b755c7dfe.html|access-date=August 3, 2020}}</ref> McMaster's attorneys argued that the decision to use emergency funds for private schools should be decided by the voters or the [[South Carolina General Assembly]], not a court order.<ref name=":5" /> On October 7, in the case ''Adams v. Henry McMaster'', the [[South Carolina Supreme Court]] unanimously struck down McMaster's decision, citing a violation of the state constitution.<ref>{{Cite web|last=South Carolina Supreme Court|date=October 7, 2020|title=Dr. Thomasena Adams, Rhonda Polin, Shaun Thacker, Orangeburg County School District, Sherry East, and the South Carolina Education Association, Petitioners, v. Governor Henry McMaster, Palmetto Promise Institute, South Carolina Office of the Treasurer, and South Carolina Department of Administration, Respondents.|url=https://www.sccourts.org/opinions/HTMLFiles/SC/28000.pdf}}</ref><ref name="wistv-10-2020" /> On October 23, McMaster filed a petition to the court to review the ruling.<ref name="wistv-10-2020">{{Cite news|last=Raven|first=Jason|date=October 23, 2020|title=McMaster petitions SC Supreme Court to rehear SAFE Grants case|work=WISTV|url=https://www.wistv.com/2020/10/23/mcmaster-petitions-sc-supreme-court-rehear-safe-grants-case/|access-date=October 23, 2020}}</ref>

====Comments about Democrats====

In May 2023, McMaster angered Democrats after saying at a convention at [[River Bluff High School]]: "I look forward to the day that Democrats are so rare, we have to hunt them with dogs." Democrats demanded an apology and compared the comments to [[slave catcher]]s who would use dogs to hunt escaped slaves.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Conception |first1=Summer |title=South Carolina governor faces backlash for remark about hunting Democrats with dogs |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/politics-news/democrats-blasted-gov-mcmaster-remark-hunting-dogs-rcna85744 |website=nbcnews.com |publisher=NBC News |access-date=3 June 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Snipes |first1=Anisa |title='Hunt them with dogs': Gov. McMaster's office responds after comment gets backlash |url=https://www.foxcarolina.com/2023/05/23/hunt-them-with-dogs-gov-mcmasters-office-responds-after-comment-gets-backlash/ |website=foxcarolina.com |publisher=FOX Carolina |access-date=3 June 2023}}</ref> A spokesperson for McMaster said that he "has been making this joke at GOP conventions for years, and everyday South Carolinians understand that it's a joke."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mueller |first=Julia |date=2023-05-22 |title=South Carolina Democrats demand apology from GOP governor for 'hunt them with dogs' comment |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/state-watch/4014994-south-carolina-democrats-demand-apology-from-gop-governor-for-hunt-them-with-dogs-comment/ |access-date=2023-05-22 |website=The Hill |language=en-US}}</ref>

====Kidnapping plot allegations====
{{See also|Gretchen Whitmer kidnapping plot}}
On October 8, 2020, a federal indictment against six men associated with the Wolverine Watchmen, a Michigan-based [[American militia movement|militia group]], was unsealed. The indictment charges the men with plotting to kidnap Michigan governor [[Gretchen Whitmer]] and violently overthrow Michigan's government.<ref>{{Cite news|last1=Bogel-Burroughs |first1=Nicholas |last2=Dewan|first2=Shaila |last3=Gray|first3=Kathleen |date=October 8, 2020 |title=F.B.I. Says Michigan Militia Plotted to Kidnap Gov. Gretchen Whitmer|language=en-US|work=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/10/08/us/gretchen-whitmer-michigan-militia.html |access-date=October 13, 2020 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> A search warrant unsealed on October 28 revealed that the suspects had also discussed targeting McMaster during the early stages of their planning. One of the suspects was arrested in [[Columbia, South Carolina|Columbia]] at the time the plot was uncovered, but there was no indication that he had plans of going after McMaster.<ref name=Detroit.AlsoWanted>{{cite news|url=https://www.detroitnews.com/story/news/local/michigan/2020/10/28/militia-member-helped-feds-infiltrate-whitmer-kidnap-crew-fbi-says/6060368002/|title=Whitmer kidnap plotter also wanted to hang Trump, other politicians, FBI says|publisher=The Detroit News|last1=Snell|first1=Robert|last2=Mauger|first2=Craig|last3=Hunter|first3=George|date=October 28, 2020|access-date=October 28, 2020}}</ref><ref name=PostAndCourier.Target>{{cite news|url=https://www.postandcourier.com/columbia/news/sc-gov-mcmaster-was-target-of-group-accused-of-plotting-to-kidnap-michigan-governor/article_36a665a4-196d-11eb-a91c-0ffded180433.html|title=SC Gov. McMaster was target of group accused of plotting to kidnap Michigan governor|publisher=The Post and Courier|last1=Shain|first1=Andy|last2=Cranney|first2=Joey|date=October 28, 2020|access-date=October 28, 2020}}</ref>

====Conservation and preservation====

McMaster has been an advocate of preserving the [[Gullah]] culture, an [[African American]] ethnic group who predominantly live in South Carolina's [[Lowcountry]] region.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Kukulich |first1=Tony |title=McMaster speaks out against St. Helena Island changes, fears 'open season' for developers |url=https://www.postandcourier.com/hilton-head/mcmaster-speaks-out-against-st-helena-island-changes-fears-open-season-for-developers/article_3b0a6754-8c3d-11ed-8d80-af8f9ba1def5.html |website=postandcourier.com |publisher=The Post and Courier |access-date=19 June 2023}}</ref> In 2022, he declared the first week in August to be Gullah/Geechee appreciation week,<ref>{{cite web |last1=McMaster |first1=Henry |title=Governor's Proclamation |url=https://i0.wp.com/gullahgeecheenation.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/07/SC-Gullah-Geechee-Nation-Appreciation-Week-2022.jpg?ssl=1 |access-date=19 June 2023}}</ref> and awarded the [[Order of the Palmetto]] award to [[Marquetta Goodwine|Queen Quet]], the chieftess of the Gullah/Geeche nation.<ref>{{cite web |title=Gullah/Geechee Nation |url=https://gullahgeecheenation.com/2022/07/28/governor-of-south-carolina-proclaims-gullah-geechee-nation-appreciation-week-statewide/ |website=gullahgeecheenation.com |access-date=19 June 2023}}</ref> McMaster has also called for the preservation of South Carolina's environmental landscapes from "over-development, mismanagement, flooding, erosion or from storm damage."<ref>{{cite web |last1=McMaster |first1=Henry |title=Inaugural Address As Prepared for Delivery |url=https://governor.sc.gov/news/2023-01/inaugural-address-prepared-delivery-0 |website=governor.sc.gov |access-date=21 June 2023}}</ref> He argued that such conservation would preserve South Carolina's culture and the economic revenue generated from such landscapes.<ref>{{cite web |title=Editorial: There's a business case for conservation. Gov McMaster just made it. |url=https://www.postandcourier.com/opinion/editorials/editorial-theres-a-business-case-for-conservation-gov-mcmaster-just-made-it/article_2e510b14-a0c7-11ed-adac-4f963e1f61bb.html |publisher=The Post and Courier |access-date=21 June 2023}}</ref>

