==Personal life==

=== Family ===
McMaster has been married to Peggy McMaster ({{née}} McAbee) since March 18, 1978.<ref name="manual2025gov">{{cite book |title=2025 South Carolina Legislative Manual |editor-last=Reid |editor-first=Charles F. |chapter=The Governor |chapter-url= |page=16 |access-date=October 11, 2026}}</ref> They have two adult children and reside in [[Columbia, South Carolina]]. He owns an [[Bulldog|English Bulldog]] named Mac. McMaster and his family attend [[First Presbyterian Church (Columbia, South Carolina)|First Presbyterian Church]] in Columbia.<ref>{{Cite web|title=The First Lady {{!}} S.C. Governor Henry McMaster|url=https://governor.sc.gov/first-lady|access-date=November 18, 2020|website=governor.sc.gov}}</ref>

===Properties===
McMaster and his wife own several houses and rental apartments in the Columbia area. One home they own is the [[McCord House]], which was constructed in 1849 and used as a Union headquarters during the Civil War.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.free-times.com/news/local-and-state-news/henry-mcmaster-s-columbia-properties-continue-to-collect-code-violations/article_ed04a9bc-c16c-11e6-97e8-9f7297985e58.html|title=Henry McMaster's Columbia properties continue to collect code violations|first=Andy|last=Shain|work=[[Free Times (Columbia)|Free Times]]|access-date=April 27, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.free-times.com/archives/henry-mcmaster-slumlord-millionaire/article_99f60927-21d2-5865-9b5f-84b050631496.html|title=Henry McMaster: Slumlord Millionaire?|first=Corey|last=Hutchins|work=Free Times|access-date=April 27, 2017}}</ref> McMaster typically leases or rents these homes to [[University of South Carolina]] students; they are his largest source of income.<ref name="Mold" /> According to the [[Post and Courier]], tenants have complained that the properties are unsafe and unsanitary. Images of roaches, outdated utilities, mold, bedbugs, mice, and bats validated the claim. Tenants have also complained about mold and having to wait months for pest control. When asked about the issues, McMaster denied the homes were unfit for renting. A spokeswoman for his campaign said, "Of course they are livable. No one would rent them if they were not."<ref name="Mold">{{cite web |last1=Cranney |first1=Joseph |title=At one of Gov. McMaster's SC rental properties, mold, roaches and a stabbing |url=https://www.postandcourier.com/news/at-one-of-gov-mcmasters-sc-rental-properties-mold-roaches-and-a-stabbing/article_dcfc0134-d09a-11e8-a688-1b6490489a8d.html |publisher=The Post and Courier}}</ref> In 2016, McMaster's tax return showed that he received $7.7 million in rent on such houses and apartments between 2006 and 2015; he paid a little over $500,000 for upkeep, maintenance, and cleaning. McMaster has also reportedly paid over $16,000 in fines for ordinance violations.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.thestate.com/news/politics-government/article142126869.html|title=What your landlord, the governor, paid to keep you comfy|work=The State|access-date=April 27, 2017}}</ref> He has suggested that the fines were accrued because tenants trashed the properties.<ref name="Mold" />

==Electoral history==
{{Main|Electoral history of Henry McMaster}}

McMaster ran unsuccessfully for the [[U.S. Senate]] in [[1986 United States Senate election in South Carolina|1986]], for lieutenant governor in 1990, and for governor in [[2010 South Carolina gubernatorial election#Republican primary|2010]].<ref name="Collins1" /> He was elected attorney general in 2002 and 2006, lieutenant governor in [[2014 South Carolina elections#Lieutenant Governor|2014]], and governor in [[2018 South Carolina gubernatorial election|2018]]<ref>{{cite web |title=2018 Statewide General Elections, detailed results |url=https://www.enr-scvotes.org/SC/92124/242328/reports/detailxml.zip |publisher=South Carolina Election Commission |date=November 6, 2018}}</ref> and [[2022 South Carolina gubernatorial election|2022]].<ref>{{cite web |title=2022 Statewide General Elections, detailed results |url=https://www.enr-scvotes.org/SC/115412/313728/reports/detailxml.zip |publisher=South Carolina Election Commission |date=November 8, 2022}}</ref>

